      "lem:ridge-fallback-inactive",
      "lem:softmax-path-entropy"
    ],
    "justification": "One shared multiplier path preserves covariance across preferences and all stacked coordinates, while the product-bias, sum-radius, path plug-in, and softmax-entropy bounds make the fold-specific plug-in step auditable.",
    "gap": "BelloniChernozhukovChetverikovWei2018 validates estimated-score multipliers generically; the new theorem verifies its replacement and tightness requirements for the anchor's cross-fitted implicit optimizer path.",
    "consumer": "An applied researcher can compute one critical value without re-solving the policy optimization inside each bootstrap draw.",
    "proof_tex": "Write\n\\[\n \\Psi_i^0(c)=\\Psi_c(O_i)-\\overline\\Psi_n(c),\\qquad\n \\widetilde\\Psi_i(c)=\\Psi_i^0(c)+\\Delta_i(c).\n\\]\nThe true class is uniformly bounded and Lipschitz by (29).  Applying the ordinary scalar law of large numbers on a finite grid and using the Lipschitz bound between grid points gives\n\\[\n \\sup_{c,t}\\left\\|n^{-1}\\sum_i\\Psi_i^0(c)\\Psi_i^0(t)^\\top-\\Gamma(c,t)\\right\\|\n \\longrightarrow_{\\mathbb P}0.                                \\tag{33}\n\\]\nCentering causes no extra term because\n\\(\\sup_c\\|\\overline\\Psi_n(c)\\|=O_{\\mathbb P}(n^{-1/2})\\), by the same grid argument.  Cauchy--Schwarz and the definition of \\(\\delta_n\\) give\n\\[\n \\begin{split}\n \\sup_{c,t}\\left\\|n^{-1}\\sum_i\\widetilde\\Psi_i(c)\n       \\widetilde\\Psi_i(t)^\\top\n       -n^{-1}\\sum_i\\Psi_i^0(c)\\Psi_i^0(t)^\\top\\right\\|\n &\\leq 2\\delta_n\\sup_t\\{n^{-1}\\sum_i\\|\\Psi_i^0(t)\\|^2\\}^{1/2}+\\delta_n^2\\\\\n &=o_{\\mathbb P}(1),                                           \\tag{34}\n \\end{split}\n\\]\nbecause the middle supremum is bounded in probability and the resolved replacement theorem gives \\(\\delta_n=o_{\\mathbb P}(1)\\).  Equations (33)--(34) prove uniform covariance consistency.\n\nConsider the oracle centered multiplier process\n\\[\n G_n^0(c)=n^{-1/2}\\sum_i\\xi_i\\Psi_i^0(c).\n\\]\nConditionally on the observations, every finite vector of evaluations of \\(G_n^0\\) is exactly Gaussian.  Its conditional covariance converges to the corresponding block of \\(\\Gamma\\) by (33), hence its finite-dimensional conditional laws converge to those of \\(G\\).  Moreover (29) implies, with probability tending to one,\n\\[\n \\{n^{-1}\\sum_i\\|\\Psi_i^0(c)-\\Psi_i^0(t)\\|^2\\}^{1/2}\n \\leq C|c-t|.                                                   \\tag{35}\n\\]\nA dyadic-grid Gaussian chaining calculation identical to the derivation of (9), now using (35), makes the conditional oscillation over \\(|c-t|\\leq r\\) tend to zero uniformly as \\(r\\downarrow0\\).  This is conditional tightness in the separable space\n\\(C(\\mathcal C)^{d+4}\\).  Finite-dimensional convergence plus this tightness proves\n\\[\n d_{\\mathrm{BL}}\\{\\mathcal L_\\xi(G_n^0\\mid O_1^n),\\mathcal L(G)\\}\n \\longrightarrow_{\\mathbb P}0.                                \\tag{36}\n\\]\nOn the localization and curvature event used to obtain (35), the empirical optimizer is unique and its implicit path is continuous, so both \\(G_n^0\\) and \\(G_n^*\\) are genuine random elements of this continuous-path space.  The complement has probability tending to zero; assigning distance one there, or equivalently using any continuous zero extension there, leaves convergence in probability in (36) unchanged.\n\nThe actual process differs by\n\\[\n G_n^*(c)-G_n^0(c)=n^{-1/2}\\sum_i\\xi_i\\Delta_i(c).\n\\]\nThe final bound of \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}softmax\\mbox{-}path\\mbox{-}entropy}\\), together with the quantitative radius in the resolved replacement theorem, shows that the conditional expected sup norm of this difference is \\(o_{\\mathbb P}(1)\\).  Conditional Markov's inequality and the defining Lipschitz property of bounded-Lipschitz test functions transfer (36) from \\(G_n^0\\) to \\(G_n^*\\).  This proves the asserted conditional law.\n\nLastly, (31)--(32) prove \\(P(\\mathcal E_n)\\to1\\), and on that event the totalized inverse and ordinary empirical inverse coincide for all \\(c\\).  Thus the covariance calculation and multiplier calculation use one common, everywhere-defined influence stack and have the claimed limiting law.",
